WEBVTT

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:03.997
Black mat or grey mat?

00:00:04.277 --> 00:00:08.657
They look alike from across the room, but they protect completely different things.

00:00:09.257 --> 00:00:13.643
Without a mat, dropped tools dent the worktop and polished parts get scratched.

00:00:13.923 --> 00:00:18.199
With the wrong mat, sensitive electronics can still be damaged by static.

00:00:18.799 --> 00:00:22.656
A rubber mat, 4 to 6 millimetres thick, absorbs impact,

00:00:22.656 --> 00:00:26.232
stops parts rolling and protects machined surfaces.

00:00:26.512 --> 00:00:28.092
It is for mechanical work.

00:00:28.372 --> 00:00:34.767
An ESD mat, 2 millimetres thick, safely drains static charge to earth through its grounding cord.

00:00:35.047 --> 00:00:36.478
It is for electronics.

00:00:36.758 --> 00:00:42.774
Never use a rubber mat where you need ESD protection; it is not designed to drain static.

00:00:43.374 --> 00:00:49.687
Furnisys supplies black rubber mats, and grey ESD mats with a wrist strap, grounding cords,

00:00:49.687 --> 00:00:53.780
press-stud buttons, a grounding plug and a 3-way connector.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:59.961
ESD accessories also come with every electrostatic laminate worktop and shelf.

00:01:00.561 --> 00:01:03.784
One protects your parts, the other protects your circuits.

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:05.054
Which do you need?

00:01:05.334 --> 00:01:10.607
Tell us in the comments, and next, discover what ESD really is and why it matters.

00:01:11.207 --> 00:01:15.452
Furnisys: complete workspace systems, planned around your process.

